\description{
Computes a z-scored informational value (infoVal) for each
producer's classification image, using a reference distribution
matched to that producer's trial count. Handles both 2IFC and
Brief-RC paradigms with a single function: the difference is
entirely in what the user passes as \code{noise_matrix}, not in how
the statistic is computed.
}
\details{
The observed statistic is the Frobenius norm of producer j's
classification image mask, optionally restricted to a logical
\code{mask} (e.g. an oval face region via \code{\link[=make_face_mask]{make_face_mask()}}):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{norm_j = sqrt(sum( signal_matrix[mask, j]^2 ))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The reference distribution for producer j is built by simulating
\code{iter} random masks at the same trial count \code{trial_counts[j]},
mirroring Schmitz, Rougier, & Yzerbyt (2024)'s \code{genMask()}
construction:
\enumerate{
\item Sample \code{trial_counts[j]} stimulus ids uniformly from
\code{1:n_pool} (without replacement when
\code{trial_counts[j] <= n_pool}).
\item Sample \code{trial_counts[j]} responses uniformly from \verb{\{-1, +1\}}
(always with replacement).
\item Collapse \code{response} by \code{stim} via \code{mean()}.
\item Build the mask:
\code{noise_matrix[, unique_stims] \%*\% mean_response / n_unique_stims}.
\item Apply \code{mask} (if supplied) and compute the Frobenius norm.
}

Per-producer z-score:
\code{(norm_j - median(ref)) / mad(ref)}. Producers sharing a trial
count share a reference; this makes the simulation efficient.

Choice of statistic: the \strong{Frobenius norm} is the correct form
per Schmitz, Rougier, & Yzerbyt (2020 comment + 2020 erratum).
The original \code{rcicr::computeInfoVal2IFC()} already uses it
(\code{norm(matrix(target_ci[["ci"]]), "f")}); this function matches
that convention for both 2IFC and Brief-RC data.

Trial-count matching: the original
\code{rcicr::generateReferenceDistribution2IFC()} builds the
reference using the full pool size. For Brief-RC the producer's
recorded \code{n_trials} is typically smaller than \code{n_pool}, so a
pool-size reference biases infoVal downward. \code{infoval()} closes
this gap by keying the reference on the actual per-producer
recorded trial count.

What infoVal measures (and what it does not). The Frobenius
norm is a \strong{magnitude} statistic, not a target-alignment one.
Cross-paradigm comparisons need care; stability and
discriminability are different questions and call for
\code{\link[=rel_split_half]{rel_split_half()}} / \code{\link[=rel_icc]{rel_icc()}} / \code{\link[=rel_cluster_test]{rel_cluster_test()}} /
\code{\link[=rel_dissimilarity]{rel_dissimilarity()}}.
